WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
We're accustomed to a very specific
relationship with artificial intelligence.

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.280
You type a question into a chat box and
the

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.960
system types an answer back.

4
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.320
It operates as a closed loop, isolated
from the outside

5
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.160
world. Safety testing usually reflects
this setup by focusing on

6
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.240
screening the user's text prompts.

7
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.360
When models pass these isolated
single-turn evaluations, AI developers report

8
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.480
high safety scores as proof the system is
secure.

9
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.240
But modern models are rarely deployed as
isolated chatbots.

10
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.480
They operate as autonomous agents, wired
into external tools, live

11
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.720
databases, and code environments.

12
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.880
The Failure First project tested these
systems at scale, evaluating

13
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.360
120 models with over 18,000 prompts
designed to probe these

14
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.720
structures. This research was designed to
see if the safety

15
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.080
methods used for isolated chat windows
still hold up when

16
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.200
the model is plugged into a wider software
ecosystem.

17
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.720
The first architectural layer to consider
is the input.

18
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.760
In an agentic system, the model ingests
instructions from multiple

19
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.520
sources simultaneously, and the user's
prompt is only one part

20
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:07.640
of that stream.

21
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.520
When an AI agent connects to a new plugin,
it

22
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.720
reads a skill file to understand how that
tool works.

23
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.080
Attackers can use supply chain injection
too tirely.

24
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:21.560
Across six different small open-weight
models, hiding instructions inside these

25
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.840
skill files achieved an attack success
rate between 90 and

26
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.320
100%. This happens because standard safety
training focuses on the

27
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.680
user input channel.

28
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.040
The AI treats information arriving through
the tool loading channel

29
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.240
as a legitimate instruction, allowing the
payload to reach the

30
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.000
model's core unobstructed.

31
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.920
In an agentic pipeline, any external
plugin or skill definition

32
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.960
becomes a potential vector for a hostile
takeover.

33
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.600
The second architectural layer is the
output.

34
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.320
While a typical chatbot responds in
conversational text, an agentic

35
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.600
model often communicates with other
software using structured data formats

36
00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.680
like JSON or YAML.

37
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.040
Attackers exploit this using format lock
attacks.

38
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.640
They instruct the AI to generate harmful
content but demand

39
00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.520
it be structured strictly within JSON
code.

40
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.880
This reveals what researchers call the
faithfulness gap.

41
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.000
The models focus so heavily on the complex
task of

42
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.040
formatting the code correctly that they
fail to filter the

43
00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.080
actual text they are slotting into the
data fields.

44
00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.440
This technique is effective even against
frontier models.

45
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.160
Claude Sonnet 4.5 showed a 30% failure
rate, Gemini 3

46
00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.840
Flash was at 24%, and Codex GPT 5.2
reached 42%.

47
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.400
Relying on conversational text for safety
evaluations means missing the

48
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.520
dangerous payloads embedded inside
structured code.

49
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.360
The third layer is memory.

50
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.720
A common assumption in the industry
suggests that more capable

51
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.920
reasoning models are inherently more
resilient to manipulation than simpler

52
00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:57.400
ones. To test this, researchers used
crescendo attacks, a method

53
00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.080
that exploits an AI's memory by slowly
escalating the severity

54
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.760
of requests across a long, multi-turn
conversation.

55
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.000
The data shows a sharp contrast.

56
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.200
Small models often forget the earlier
context and fail the

57
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.040
attack at a 10% rate.

58
00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:15.960
However, against a reasoning model like
DeepSeek R1, the success

59
00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.320
rate climbs to between 80 and 90% as the
conversation

60
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.320
length grows. A reasoning model's ability
to perfectly track context

61
00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.720
is exactly what an attacker weaponizes.

62
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.120
Because the model remains committed to the
logic of the

63
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.680
previous turn, it is more likely to be led
toward

64
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:31.960
a harmful conclusion.

65
00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:35.240
For an AI agent, a larger context window
also means

66
00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.000
an expanded attack surface.

67
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.720
These vulnerabilities suggest a logical
question.

68
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.080
Why haven't these issues appeared in
standard safety benchmarks?

69
00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.560
The problem lies in how safety tests are
graded.

70
00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:52.080
Most automated benchmarks use
keyword-based classification, scanning the AI's response

71
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.520
for specific trigger words to decide if an
attack worked.

72
00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.520
The Failure First study found that these
keyword heuristics inflate

73
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.920
the reported risk of simple attacks by
roughly 2.3 times.

74
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.400
Keyword graders have an 88% false positive
rate, often labeling

75
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.600
a response as a successful attack even
when the model

76
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:15.840
refused. Correcting these errors with LLM
grading caused the aggregate

77
00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.120
attack success rate to drop from 36.2% to
15.9%.

78
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.960
This creates a blind spot.

79
00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.440
Benchmarks over-report the risk of
conversational text attacks while failing

80
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.320
to detect the real threats hidden in JSON
formats and

81
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:31.920
external tool files.

82
00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.280
The Failure First dataset shows that
securing a modern model

83
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.720
requires moving past the single text
prompt and testing the

84
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.240
full interconnected ar.chitecture

